PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Current consensus remains F. Horizontal flip augmentation. All four colleagues now explicitly support F, including the former C supporter. Key rationale: fixed side-on camera at pinch point means animals can pass left-to-right or right-to-left; species identity is left-right invariant; GBIF training images may have orientation bias. This is label-preserving and directly targets a real deployment variation.

Main competing concern still noted but judged weaker: GBIF visible-light/daylight images vs nighttime fixed-brightness IR deployment. C (non-linear luminance tone-curve) is tempting for tonal/contrast differences, but does not simulate IR grayscale/spectral properties and leaves color cues; deployment brightness is fixed, so brightness augmentation is less targeted. Linear brightness, color/noise/swizzle, Gaussian blur, motion blur, scale, vertical flip are weaker: 1/500s makes motion blur unlikely; ISO 190 limits noise; fixed camera/focus constrains scale/blur; vertical flips are unnatural; brightness/color augmentations do not address true IR mismatch.

Unresolved: no listed augmentation properly simulates visible-light-to-IR domain shift. A grayscale/IR-style augmentation could theoretically be valuable, but it is not an option. Whether GBIF images are already orientation-balanced is uncertain, but horizontal flip remains useful regardless. Current best answer: F.}

